    "Stem": "Sterling Equipment's IT department applies emergency patches to its ERP system 8 to 10 times per year. The current emergency-patch procedure is: the IT manager identifies a required patch, spends one day testing it in the development environment, then deploys it to the production system over the weekend. There is no formal change request, no documented approval, no rollback plan, and no post-implementation review. The last two emergency patches caused a combined 14 hours of unplanned production downtime. The IT manager argues that the urgency of closing security vulnerabilities justifies bypassing formal change management, and that one day of testing is sufficient because the vendor certifies all patches. Evaluate this situation against COSO Principle 11: is this a significant IT general control deficiency, and what minimum change management controls should apply even to emergency patches?",
