    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under COSO Principle 11, IT general controls include change management processes that govern how modifications to technology infrastructure are authorized, tested, approved, and deployed. The current emergency-patch procedure at Sterling Equipment has three critical gaps. First, the IT manager unilaterally identifies, tests, and deploys patches with no independent authorization — a single point of control failure that violates segregation principles. Second, the absence of a documented rollback plan means that if a patch causes system failure (as occurred twice, producing 14 hours of downtime), there is no pre-approved, tested method to restore the prior stable state. Third, the lack of post-implementation review means the organization cannot learn from failures — the same root causes may recur because no analysis is performed. However, emergency patches differ from routine changes in their urgency and should not be subject to the identical full change management process. A properly designed emergency change procedure provides a streamlined but still controlled path: a second party (such as the CIO or IT security lead) must authorize the emergency deployment, a rollback plan must be documented and tested before the patch is applied, and a post-implementation review must be completed within a defined period (typically 24 to 48 hours) to assess whether the patch succeeded, caused unintended effects, or requires additional remediation. This balances the urgency of security vulnerability closure with the control requirement to protect production system integrity.",
