    "ExplanationWrongA": "The urgency of deploying a security patch does not eliminate the need for basic change management controls. While it is true that unpatched vulnerabilities expose the organization to external threats, the scenario provides concrete evidence that the absence of controls has already caused material harm: 14 hours of production ERP downtime across two emergency deployments. One day of testing in a development environment, without a second reviewer and without a rollback plan, proved insufficient in both cases. Furthermore, vendor certification of a patch does not guarantee compatibility with Sterling Equipment's specific ERP configuration — customizations, integrations, and the unique operating environment can cause a vendor-certified patch to fail in production. The argument that urgency trumps control confuses the need for a streamlined emergency process (which is legitimate) with the elimination of all process (which is not). COSO Principle 11 does not require the same process for all changes, but it does require that some process exists for all changes, including emergency ones.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Post-deployment documentation alone — essentially a log entry recording what was done after the fact — provides retrospective visibility but offers no prospective protection. By the time the log is written, the patch has already been deployed; if it caused system failure, the damage has occurred. Documentation without approval means there was no independent judgment applied before deployment. Documentation without a rollback plan means that when the system goes down, the IT team is improvising a recovery rather than executing a pre-tested procedure. The two combined outages totaling 14 hours demonstrate that post-hoc logging does not prevent or mitigate deployment failures. Effective change management requires controls before implementation (authorization, testing, rollback planning) as well as after (review, documentation). Limiting remediation to documentation alone addresses only the retrospective component while leaving the prospective risks entirely unmanaged.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Emergency patches and routine changes do not carry equal risk profiles, and COSO does not require identical processes for all change types. Routine changes — such as a scheduled module upgrade or a new report deployment — typically allow weeks of planning, multi-environment testing, and formal change advisory board review. Emergency security patches address actively exploited vulnerabilities and may require deployment within hours. Applying the same full governance process to emergency patches would create an unacceptable delay that leaves the organization exposed to known threats. The appropriate governance response is a tiered change management framework: a full process for routine changes and an expedited but still controlled process for emergency changes. The expedited process preserves the essential control elements (independent authorization, rollback planning, post-implementation review) while eliminating steps that can be safely deferred (formal board presentation, extended testing cycles). This option is incorrect because it treats all changes as equal, which would paralyze the organization's ability to respond to security threats.",
