    "ExplanationWrongB": "Eighteen months is more than sufficient time for a control framework to be absorbed by an organization of Northfield Industries' size. The survey results are not a reflection of insufficient time — they are direct evidence of a communication failure. Three-quarters of employees cannot name a single control responsibility a year and a half after implementation, and nearly two-thirds have never received any training whatsoever. A 45-page manual distributed once via email to department heads, with no acknowledgment mechanism, no training sessions, and no follow-up, is not adequate communication under any interpretation of COSO Principle 14. The principle explicitly requires that communication enables personnel to understand internal control responsibilities; the survey demonstrates the opposite. Furthermore, management's approach places the entire communication burden on department heads, who may lack the time, expertise, or incentive to effectively cascade control information to their teams — and management performed no verification to determine whether cascading occurred. Treating demonstrable communication failure as a timing issue rather than a process deficiency would delay remediation indefinitely while the control gap persists.",
